  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 949 PM MDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nA potent cold front is pushing through the Borderland this evening, \nbringing strong NE winds behind it. Gusts over 50 mph have \nalready been seen E of the US 54 corridor. The strength of the \nsfc high behind the front is more in-line with what we tend to see\nin the middle of winter (1045mb-ish over central high plains on \nFri). This will result in an impressive pressure gradient across \nthe CWA as the front sweeps through in the near-term. West mtn \nslopes will see the gustiest winds, reaching 60 mph or higher. The\nstrongest winds are expected to occur from now through early Fri \nAM with winds easing a bit during the day on Fri and shifting \nmore easterly. Generally 25-35 mph sustained winds are modeled \nwith a pocket of lighter winds from Las Cruces up towards Holloman\nAFB. No changes have been made to the wind headlines. Forecast \nremains on track.\n\nDust models, including the RRFS and NOAA AQ model, show a signal for \nblowing dust through the overnight, favoring S Otero County and far \nwest TX. Low chance of 1 mile VIS in isolated areas, most likely in \nEl Paso County. Any dust lifted off White Sands should get blocked \nby the San Andres and struggle to reach the upper RGV. The dust \nthreat lowers Fri AM and shifts out W towards the Lordsburg Playa\nwhere winds remain strong. Confidence is lower in seeing dust \nimpacts in Hidalgo Co compared to eastern areas, where BLDU \nAdvisories are in effect until 9am Fri.\n\nAn upper low cuts off from the flow off the Baja coast for the rest \nof the week, pulling moisture into it. A ridge of high pressure \nextends from the NW at the same time, keeping most of the moisture \nto our south. Precip chances remain near zero into the weekend as \nwinds subside through Sat. The upper low opens up into a shortwave \non Sun, bringing some of its moisture into the region and slight \nrain chances for western areas. Then, a Pacific storm system dives \ndown the West Coast, pulling more subtropical moisture northward \nthrough midweek from the Pacific. PWs climb towards 1\" with low-\nmedium rain chances area-wide late in the period. A low flood \nthreat is present as NBM 90th %-ile shows 24-hr rain amounts of \n1-2\" Wed and Thu. Confidence is low in impacts for this midweek \nrain event.\n\nTemperatures crash to unseasonably cool levels (highs 10-20 degrees \nbelow normal) behind tonight's front. Lowland highs in the low-mid \n60s are forecast through Sat with cold air advection ongoing. It'll \nfeel more like winter than early fall, especially in the Sacs Fri AM \nas wind chills reach the teens. Lowland areas to the N and E are\nlikely to dip into the 30s Sat AM with patchy frost. Temperatures\nrebound towards normal early next week.",
